        {
          "title": "A Vulnerability in Libsodium",
          "url": "https://00f.net/2025/12/30/libsodium-vulnerability/",
          "source": "Hacker News",
          "date": "2025-12-30T17:24:57Z",
          "language": "en",
          "section": "us_news",
          "raw_content": "I started that project to pursue Dan Bernsteinâs desire to make cryptography simple to use. That meant exposing a limited set of high-level functions and parameters, providing a simple API, and writing documentation for users, not cryptographers. Libsodiumâs goal was to expose APIs to perform operations, not low-level functions. Users shouldnât even have to know or care about what algorithms are used internally. This is how Iâve always viewed libsodium. Never breaking the APIs is also something Iâm obsessed with. APIs may not be great, and if I could start over from scratch, I would have made them very different, but as a developer, the best APIs are not the most beautifully designed ones, but the ones that you donât have to worry about because they donât change and upgrades donât require any changes in your application either. Libsodium started from the NaCl API, and sti",
